 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The Douglas-Peucker algorithm typically runs in $O(n \log n)$ on average, where $n$ is the number of histogram bins, though worst-case is $O(n^2)$. Sorting peaks is $O(k \log k)$ where $k$ is the number of peaks.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(n)$ to store the histogram and polyline points.
-*   **Strengths:** Reduces complex transfer function design to a few simple, recognizable components. Automates the discovery of major material boundaries.
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the assumption that structures correspond to histogram peaks. The linear opacity model is restrictive and data-type dependent. The choice of $\epsilon$ significantly impacts the result and requires user tuning.
-*   **When to use:** When the dataset has distinct material classes with clear density separations (e.g., medical CT).
-*   **When not to use:** When the data lacks clear histogram peaks or when the density-to-opacity relationship is non-linear or inverted.
